**Tested at:** `174c0fd38990c326c6a4ad00b20a492b1af2619a` (main, 2026-11-19)

## What happens

`POST /keys/provision` advertises P2PKH (`1...`) support, and P2PKH wallets sign with BIP-137. In `src/services/btc-verify.ts`, `verifyBip137` always recovers the **compressed** public key (`recoveredPoint.toBytes(true)`, line 272) and derives the P2PKH address from it, whatever the header byte says.

BIP-137 headers 27-30 mean "P2PKH, **uncompressed** key": the address is HASH160 of the 65-byte public key. So:

1. A valid signature from an uncompressed-key P2PKH address (old wallets, paper wallets, early addresses) is **rejected**. The derived address is the compressed one, so it never matches: `INVALID_SIGNATURE` for a correct signature.
2. The other way round, a header-27 signature is **accepted** for the *compressed*-key address of the same key. Bitcoin Core's `verifymessage` returns false for that, because the header commits to the uncompressed key.

## What should happen

For header 27-30, derive the address from the uncompressed public key. For 31-42, use the compressed key, as now. That matches BIP-137 and Bitcoin Core.

## Reproduce

Drop this file into `src/__tests__/` and run `npx vitest run src/__tests__/btc-verify-bip137-uncompressed.test.ts`. The first case is a control: a compressed key with header 31 passes today. The other two fail on `174c0fd`.

```ts
import { describe, expect, it } from "vitest";
import { secp256k1 } from "@noble/curves/secp256k1.js";
import { p2pkh } from "@scure/btc-signer";
import { hashSha256Sync } from "@stacks/encryption";
import { BtcVerifyService, BTC_MESSAGES } from "../services/btc-verify";
import type { Logger } from "../types";

const logger = { debug() {}, info() {}, warn() {}, error() {} } as unknown as Logger;

// BIP-137 message hash: dsha256("\x18Bitcoin Signed Message:\n" || varint(len) || msg)
function bip137Hash(message: string): Uint8Array {
  const prefix = new TextEncoder().encode("\x18Bitcoin Signed Message:\n");
  const msg = new TextEncoder().encode(message);
  const buf = new Uint8Array(prefix.length + 1 + msg.length);
  buf.set(prefix, 0);
  buf[prefix.length] = msg.length; // < 0xfd
  buf.set(msg, prefix.length + 1);
  return hashSha256Sync(hashSha256Sync(buf));
}

// Sign the way a legacy wallet does: header = base + recovery id.
function signBip137(priv: Uint8Array, message: string, headerBase: 27 | 31): string {
  const rec = secp256k1.sign(bip137Hash(message), priv, { prehash: false, format: "recovered" });
  const out = new Uint8Array(65);
  out[0] = headerBase + rec[0];
  out.set(rec.slice(1), 1);
  return Buffer.from(out).toString("base64");
}

const priv = new Uint8Array(32).fill(7);

describe("BIP-137 P2PKH signatures", () => {
  it("accepts a compressed-key P2PKH signature (header 31-34)", () => {
    const address = p2pkh(secp256k1.getPublicKey(priv, true)).address!;
    const sig = signBip137(priv, BTC_MESSAGES.BASE, 31);
    expect(new BtcVerifyService(logger).verify(address, BTC_MESSAGES.BASE, sig).valid).toBe(true);
  });

  it("accepts an uncompressed-key P2PKH signature (header 27-30)", () => {
    // Header 27-30 means the address hashes the 65-byte uncompressed public key.
    const address = p2pkh(secp256k1.getPublicKey(priv, false)).address!;
    const sig = signBip137(priv, BTC_MESSAGES.BASE, 27);
    expect(new BtcVerifyService(logger).verify(address, BTC_MESSAGES.BASE, sig).valid).toBe(true);
  });

  it("rejects header 27-30 against the compressed-key address of the same key", () => {
    // Header 27-30 commits to the uncompressed key, so it must not verify for the compressed address.
    const address = p2pkh(secp256k1.getPublicKey(priv, true)).address!;
    const sig = signBip137(priv, BTC_MESSAGES.BASE, 27);
    expect(new BtcVerifyService(logger).verify(address, BTC_MESSAGES.BASE, sig).valid).toBe(false);
  });
});
```

Output on `174c0fd`: `Tests 2 failed | 1 passed (3)`. The test signs with `@noble/curves` (`format: "recovered"`) and builds the address with `@scure/btc-signer`'s `p2pkh`, both already dependencies.

Scope: as far as I can see this only affects the relay's BIP-137 path, used by P2PKH and P2SH-P2WPKH provisioning. landing-page has the same derivation (`lib/bitcoin-verify.ts`), but registration there rejects legacy addresses (#410/#411), so I don't think it is reachable there.

